BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Leave Protections & Supporting Employees with Growing Families
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T123000
UID:260903rIeiYww3uEqOmQ5szPJHfQ
DESCRIPTION:Join us for this free educational seminar open to all Sonoma C
 ounty employers\, business leaders\, HR professionals\, and managers. The 
 second session of this three-part Workplace & Workforce Innovation series 
 explores the complex landscape of state and federal leave laws while highl
 ighting practical strategies that help employers support employees balanci
 ng work and family responsibilities. Presented by employment law attorney 
 Samantha Pungprakearti\, this seminar combines expert employment law guida
 nce with practical approaches to creating flexible\, equitable workplace p
 olicies that strengthen employee engagement and retention. Participants wi
 ll learn: How state and federal leave laws protect new parents and what em
 ployers need to know when employees take leave to grow their families. Pra
 ctical strategies for supporting employees navigating childcare challenges
  while maintaining business operations. How to create equitable work-life 
 balance policies that support employees with and without children\, streng
 thening engagement\, retention\, and workplace culture. The FREE series is
  presented by the Santa Rosa Metro Chamber as part of its Workplace & Work
 force Innovation initiative\, helping employers build workplaces where emp
 loyees and businesses thrive. This free series is made possible through fu
 nding from First 5 Sonoma County and Measure I. PLEASE RSVP: https://web.s
 antarosametrochamber.com/atlas/events-v4/register/2626
LOCATION:Santa Rosa Dowtown Square
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/1713339633232298/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artist's Way Group Next Chapter: "The Book of Alchemy"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T210000
UID:260519FjCnyb2Nsk-YIDdTDk-9rQ
DESCRIPTION:Creativity is my way of making sense of the world.” — Sule
 ika Jaouad  Are you feeling an urge to pick up a pen or paintbrush or pair
  of scissors and unleash your creativity\, but not sure where to start? Or
  are you looking to find community\, to motivate your creative self and ma
 ke new friends? This might be just the thing for you. Two years ago\, we f
 ormed an “Artist’s Way” group here at the shop\, and continued it ag
 ain last year. It was extraordinary. Not only did people write and paint a
 nd create\, but got published and found new life paths. \\(And even love!\
 \) But best of all\, a community was established\, offering encouragement 
 for creative endeavors\, and friendship and support. It has been life affi
 rming. Now\, in 2026\, creativity and community are more important than ev
 er\, and it’s time to gather a group again! This year\, however\, we wil
 l not be doing “The Artist’s Way\,” and instead will be exploring 
 “The Book of Alchemy” by Suleika Jaouad. This book focuses on journali
 ng and looking at our life experiences to foster creativity to inspire wha
 tever art form we choose to do.  From the publisher: “From the time she 
 was young\, Suleika Jaouad has kept a journal. She’s used it to mark lif
 e’s biggest occasions and to weather its most ferocious storms. Journali
 ng has buoyed her through illness\, heartbreak\, and the deepest uncertain
 ty. And she is not alone: for so many people\, keeping a journal is an ess
 ential tool for navigating both the personal peaks and valleys and the col
 lective challenges of modern life. More than ever\, we need a space for pu
 zzling through. In The Book of Alchemy\, Suleika explores the art of journ
 aling and shares everything she’s learned about how this life-altering p
 ractice can help us tap into that mystical trait that exists in every huma
 n: creativity. She has gathered wisdom from one hundred writers\, artists\
 , and thinkers in the form of essays and writing prompts. Their insights i
 nvite us to inhabit a more inspired life. A companion through challenging 
 times\, The Book of Alchemy is broken into themes ranging from new beginni
 ngs to love\, loss\, and rebuilding. Whether you’re a lifelong journaler
  or new to the practice\, this book gives you the tools\, direction\, and 
 encouragement to engage with discomfort\, ask questions\, peel back the la
 yers\, dream daringly\, uncover your truest self—and in doing so\, to le
 arn to hold the unbearably brutal and astonishingly beautiful facts of lif
 e in the same palm.” Beginning Tuesday\, March 3rd at 7pm through the en
 d of September \\(with flexibility\\)\, we will be meeting on Tuesdays to 
 check in and discuss the book and our progress. Each chapter has a number 
 of prompts so we will take it slow\, but also determine our pace. Every ot
 her Tuesday we will discuss the book\, and then the alternate weeks will b
 e more open — we can listen to chapters or inspiring podcasts while craf
 ting\, or just get together and talk — to give people a chance to catch 
 up or take some time. The initial meeting on March 3rd is a welcome meetin
 g for introductions\, to pick up the book\, and to set some intentions— 
 no need to read anything yet. The cost is $40 plus the purchase of the boo
 k.  To register\, come by the shop or email alibibookshop@gmail.com. Regis
 tration “closes” the end of March.  This group is truly one of the bes
 t things I have ever been a part of\, and I’m looking forward to doing i
 t again— and welcoming new folks to the fold! Hope to see you!
LOCATION:Alibi Bookshop
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/919588777104198/919588857104190/?activ
 e_tab=about
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Santa Rosa Taco Tuesday Bike Ride
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T000000
UID:260504qFjoq0-iXkGEFnqNvUT32A
DESCRIPTION:A fun community bike ride through the heart of Santa Rosa. Com
 e join us this Tuesday! We roll through the JC and McDonald Ave neighborho
 od\, stop in Courthouse Square\, then on to the taco trucks at Mitote Food
  Park in Roseland for dinner! We depart Mitote at 8:15pm\, ride back throu
 gh Courthouse Square\, then on to Yogurt Farms for dessert. This ride is 8
  miles round trip and rides all year long. Rides canceled for rain and hol
 idays. All riders welcome. Bring the whole family! Meet at SRJC parking lo
 t at 550 Silva Ave. We are renting the use of this parking lot every Tuesd
 ay from 5:30pm to 9:30 pm. Taco Tuesday riders can park in the lot during 
 that time frame for free. Please park cars along the back wall on the east
  side. And stage in the row on the northern side of the lot \\(closest to 
 Silva Ave\\). Meet at 5:30pm\; pedals up at 6pm.
LOCATION:SRJC Parking Lot
URL:https://www.visitsantarosa.com/events/#jlupcoming-events
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: Aspirations
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T180000
UID:260916GnaHIJeV3UmfDNxx46OhAQ
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  9/15/26\n\n**OCTOBER EXHIBIT: AS
 PIRATIONS**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES **– September 28 – October 25\, 2026**\n\
 n**RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, October 3\, 2026\, 3-6 pm: Join us to meet Laura 
 Roney and Linda Loveland Reid and learn about their art and inspiration. 
  Be sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthlong drawing for a beau
 tiful work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSonoma
  County artist\, **Laura Roney\,** unveils **Aspirations**\, a new collect
 ion of paintings in oil\, celebrating the unique differing goals people se
 t during our lives\, challenging us to take bold steps forward.  Of unive
 rsal inspiration**\, Aspiration and Awe** splashes the Milky Way’s starr
 y brilliance across the heavens\, capturing our imagination and hope as wo
 rldly cares are left behind.  Moving from natural forces to human endeavo
 r\, looking toward social change\, **Aspiring to Vote**\, the portrait of 
 a hopeful suffragette\, evokes the dream for a better future.  A lifetime
  goal for many\, the marathon\, **Aspiring for 26.2\,** captures the early
  morning excitement waiting for start times.  “People doing ordinary th
 ings but dreaming larger for the future has been a great inspiration for t
 his series.  I hope folks will be inspired by the boldness of people’s 
 dreams\,” commented Roney on her inspiration for the work.\n\nArt has be
 en a lifelong passion for Roney.  Beginning her art studies at a young ag
 e\, whatever avenue she was pursuing in life\, she continued her artistic 
 endeavors.  After a successful career in microbiology and pharmaceutical 
 research\, a family move to Northern California enabled Roney to focus on 
 her art.  She received her MFA from Academy of Art University\, San Franc
 isco.\n\nMeet the artist: Roney will host the Gallery on September 30 and 
 October 1 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\nThe Stairway Smallworks Showcase features 
 Linda Loveland Reid’s **Intentional Chaos\,** a collection of exciting m
 ixed-media works that explode with excitement and energy. Random patterns\
 , amorphous shapes and dancing dribbles magically coalesce into bold state
 ments.  Intriguing and compelling\, like interlocking gears\, in **Patter
 ns of Memory** multiple patterns interlock on fields of gold and black.  
 “I love throwing ink\, paint and even coffee onto a blank canvas and cre
 ating through instinct\, gesture and emotion.  Distilling energy into sma
 ller works brings new challenges and some happy surprises\,” noted Reid 
 on her creative process.\n\nMeet the artist: Reid will host the Gallery on
  October 23 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\n Also join us for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG
 **\, Thursday\, October 1\, 5 – 7 pm.  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the 1
 st Thursday of Every Month\, is a community event celebrating art and crea
 tivity with over 20 galleries participating.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\nU
 pstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Center Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 431
 -4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 pm.    **www.upstairsart gallery.net** \
 n\nFor further information: Contact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bvd
 rio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T170000
UID:260914Omy_k4wP0E27A4UtArQTyA
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present “Antarctica: The F
 rozen Desert\,” a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\, o
 pening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November.  T
 he exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after m
 ore than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity\,
  he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar regio
 ns. The exhibit features work generated over 6 voyages to the Antarctic Pe
 ninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Falklan
 ds and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photograph.\
 n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on Ear
 th\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely out
 side of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of year
 s ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 years. D
 efined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — vast
 \, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a kind
  of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever before
 .
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Raw & Rogue
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T210000
UID:260912hy-R9g-JPEqYEBtuLT0U7A
DESCRIPTION:For three Tuesdays this fall\, Raw \\+ Rogue brings an intimat
 e twelve-seat omakase experience to the rooftop of Appellation Healdsburg.
  Across twelve courses\, Chef Willer Larreal Perez—a Venezuela-born chef
  trained at omakase counters in Los Angeles and Miami—builds each dish a
 round the fish\, taking guests on a scenic culinary journey from the count
 er to the plate. An amuse opens the experience and dessert brings it to a 
 close\, while wine pairings from Baron Ziegler\, co-founder of Healdsburg
 ’s Banshee Wines and founder of Marine Layer\, move alongside the menu. 
 Each course is paired with its own song\, creating a soundtrack that build
 s plate by plate as the light shifts over Northern Sonoma County. Together
 \, the food\, wine and music create a multisensory evening designed to unf
 old in rhythm with the rooftop setting. \n\nWhen: Tuesdays | September 29
 \, October 13\, October 27 \\(6:30 pm\\) \n\nPrice: $295 per guest\, wine
  selections prices separately \n\nReservations are available through Open
 Table: Raw & Rogue \n\nPre-paid reservations are required.
LOCATION:Appellation Healdsburg
URL:https://beelinerooftop.getbento.com/event/raw-rogue/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T163000
UID:260909u9HIRF-MC0KZ1CAgldiybw
DESCRIPTION:September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Monday\, 
 Sept. 14\, the 6th California Native Creations Art Show opens Person Senio
 r Wing\, Finley Community Center\, 2060 W. College Ave.\, Santa Rosa\, CA.
  The show is free and open to the public Monday – Friday\, 8:30 a.m.-4:3
 0 p.m.\, through November 19.\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native 
 artists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in the
 ir art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The
  artists include:\n\n·         Meyo Marrufo\, painter\, guest cur
 ator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley Art Museum\,\n\n·  
        Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood Valley Rancheria\, cu
 ltural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·         Henry Edwar
 d Frank\, Yurok/Kashaya Pomo\, who will show paintings and work with Legos
  titled Mind Prison\,\n\n·         Digital artist Nicolas \\(Nico
 \\) Vargas\, Kashia Band of Pomo Indians\, whose art was featured at the 5
 5th Annual Stanford Powwow.\n\n·         plus Gloria Carretero\; 
 Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaRose\, Kash
 ia Band of Pomo Indians\; Angelica Smith\, Manchester Point Arena and Yoka
 yo Rancheria\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morgan\, Kashia Ba
 nd of Pomo Indians\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenbaugh\; Katie Wi
 lliams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, Big Valley Pomo Tribe.
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Formative Influence Art Exhibit
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T160000
UID:260909ZCAaUcRk80ai9Kvv8pKVPQ
DESCRIPTION:Formative Influence explores the transformative power of light
  across five bodies of work. Inspired by the landscapes of Northern Califo
 rnia and the American West\, the exhibition examines the shift from photog
 raphy\, where light is observed and captured\, to painting\, where light i
 s created to shape new visual experiences. Rather than documenting the lan
 dscape\, these works evoke its emotional resonance\, emphasizing atmospher
 e\, memory\, and perception over literal representation.
LOCATION:Marin Center
URL:https://www.marincultural.org/exhibitions
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
